<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  Makes the process treat narrow (char*) paths as UTF-8 instead of the machine's
  ANSI code page.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS

  Every path in this game is a std::string. It reaches fopen(), std::ifstream,
  std::filesystem::path and miniz as narrow bytes, and on Windows that means
  those bytes are interpreted in the ACTIVE ANSI CODE PAGE -- which is per
  machine and cannot represent most of Unicode.

  The installer puts the game under %LOCALAPPDATA%, i.e. C:\Users\<name>\...,
  so the player's own account name is part of every path the game opens. When
  that name contains characters the machine's code page cannot encode -- a
  Japanese or Cyrillic name on a Western-locale Windows, an emoji, an accent
  outside the page -- the bytes Windows hands back are lossy, every open fails,
  and the game starts into a world with no fonts, no maps and no scenarios. It
  does not crash and it says nothing, because each individual open merely
  returned false.

  Declaring UTF-8 makes the whole ANSI family speak UTF-8 instead: the same
  narrow calls, consistently encoded, with no code changes anywhere. It is the
  documented fix for exactly this, and it is why this is a manifest rather than
  a rewrite of every path in the project to wide strings.

  Windows 10 1903 and later honour it. Older versions ignore the setting and
  behave exactly as they do today, so this cannot make anything worse.
-->
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings
        xmlns:ws2019="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2019/WindowsSettings">
      <ws2019:activeCodePage>UTF-8</ws2019:activeCodePage>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
